Changelog: 11-10-2026 19:02:46

instance.py, the copy of core's: Two launches at the same moment (a double-click on a Desktop icon that opens on one
click) no longer open two windows. Both found no running instance, since the window and its socket take a while to
appear, and the second then removed the first one's socket. The first launch now takes a lock file at once; a launch
that finds it taken waits up to 10 s for that instance's socket and hands over instead. A crashed instance's lock is
stale, so the next launch starts as before.

Changelog: 11-10-2026 17:17:19

Release 0.2.1.

Changelog: 11-10-2026 16:03:59

Deploy page: the two settings files are explained on the page, not only in a tooltip. Under the OptiScaler.ini
lines: what OptiScaler.ini is (OptiScaler's settings file next to the game's .exe), what the lines do
(Dx12Upscaler=fsr31 hands the game's upscaling to an FSR 3.1 DLL, the two Path lines say that DLL is HelixSR in the
folder) and what Copy OptiScaler.ini lines does (the clipboard, to paste in place of the lines with the same names);
the button has a tooltip too. Under "Write helixsr.ini": what helixsr.ini is (HelixSR's own settings file next to its
DLL) and what ticked and unticked do. All languages updated.

Changelog: 11-10-2026 15:17:42

Deploy page, OptiScaler folder: the app now also installs OptiScaler, for games without a separate FSR 3.1 DLL (God of
War, DLSS / XeSS / FSR 2 games). HelixSR 1.7 and newer ship the official OptiScaler (0.9.4) with fakenvapi; importing
such a release copies its optiscaler folder into the payload. "Install OptiScaler next to the game's .exe" is on when
the payload has it, and Scan fills in the .exe folder (one that already has an OptiScaler.ini, else the folder of the
largest .exe that is no launcher, installer or crash handler; an Unreal *-Win64-Shipping.exe wins). Deploy asks
first, then puts OptiScaler there as dxgi.dll (or under the name of an OptiScaler the game already has), adds
fakenvapi where the game has none, and sets Dx12Upscaler=fsr31, FfxDx12Path and FfxDx12SRPath in its OptiScaler.ini,
keeping the file's comments and line endings. A file of the game it replaces and an OptiScaler.ini the game already
had are kept in the HelixSR folder's backup, with a record (bc250-optiscaler.json); Remove, on the Deploy page or the
Overview, puts them back and deletes what it added. Unticked, the page shows the OptiScaler.ini lines to paste, as
before. The same steps as HelixSR's own installer, for the app's folder. Help and the manual describe it, including
WINEDLLOVERRIDES="dxgi=n,b" for a game whose OptiScaler menu does not appear; all languages updated. Tests: import,
finding the .exe folder, install and remove (also over an OptiScaler the game already had), the refusals and the
Deploy page.

Changelog: 11-10-2026 15:10:06

Deploy page: in a window too small for the whole page, Qt squeezed the hints to their first line and the
OptiScaler.ini lines to one row. The Deploy and helixsr.ini pages now scroll instead; the box with the OptiScaler.ini
lines always shows all of them, and the DLL table starts smaller (it still grows with the window).

Changelog: 11-10-2026 14:06:06

Setup page: the output of HelixSR's own game installer, which runs after the build and asks about each Steam game
("GodOfWar: install? [o/d/n]"), is no longer shown, so the output no longer ends on a question nobody answers. From
its first line ("looking for games with DLSS, XeSS or FSR") on, the page says that the network is built and that
Deploy puts HelixSR into a game. Everything the build prints before that is shown as before.

Changelog: 11-10-2026 13:08:28

HelixSR 1.8.0: its setup builds the network into HelixSR's DLL (an HXSRNET1 footer at the end of the file) and removes
helixsr_weights.bin and helixsr_kernels.pak, so Download and build failed with "The setup finished but did not
produce helixsr_weights.bin, helixsr_kernels.pak". A DLL that carries the network now counts as a complete payload:
the build check, import, deploy (both modes), the deployment state and Remove accept it. Deploy copies only the DLL
and the ini, and removes network files an older release left next to it; importing such a DLL removes the payload's
old network files. The Overview shows the weights and kernels as "In the DLL". The fsr3-drop-in folder the 1.8 setup
writes is deleted after the build, like manual-install.

helixsr.ini page: HelixSR 1.8 reads sharpening again, through Sharpener (off, nvidia, rcas), Sharpness and
UseGameSlider instead of Mode. The page edits the two new keys and writes them only when they are set, so files for
older versions stay as they were. It grays out per field what the payload's version does not read: Sharpener and
UseGameSlider before 1.8, all of [Sharpening] and [ModelE] Network in 1.7, Mode and Network from 1.8 on.

The Setup page no longer checks this app's own release ("This app" with its release page link), and the status bar
no longer announces one: the app is updated through the BC250 Bazzite Suite portal. latest_release() lost its
tag-prefix search, which only that check used.

Help and the manual describe the one-file DLL, the new sharpening keys and the portal; all languages updated. Tests:
a setup that builds a one-file DLL, import and deploy of one, the 1.8 keys in the ini and the update check covering
only HelixSR.

Changelog: 10-10-2026 20:56:48

Release 0.2.0. Update to latest HelixSR release

Changelog: 10-10-2026 18:43:44

HelixSR 1.7.0: its helixsr-setup.sh now ends with a game installer that asks about every Steam game, and --yes does not
answer those questions. The app ran the script with its own input, so started from a terminal the Setup page could
wait for answers typed there. The script now gets no input: the installer stops at its first question and only the
network files are built. The manual-install folder it leaves in the work folder (another copy of the network files)
is deleted afterwards.

Setup page: Version picks the latest HelixSR release (the default) or HelixSR 1.4.3, the version HelixSR's developer
recommends when the latest one does not work. 1.4.3 is pinned by its tag (FALLBACK_TAG in acquire.py), and the
choice is saved. With the fallback as the payload, the Overview reports a newer release as information, not as an
update to install.

Deployments: after switching back to an older release, games deployed with a newer one show Newer build instead of
Older build. Stand-alone OptiScaler folders now also show Older or Newer build when their DLL is not the payload's.

helixsr.ini page: with HelixSR 1.7.0 or newer as the payload, [Sharpening] and the [ModelE] Network setting are grayed
out with a note, because those versions ignore them. Their values stay in the file for 1.4.3.

Help and the manual describe the version choice; all languages updated. Tests: the setup script gets no input and
manual-install is removed, the fallback looks up its tag, Newer build, the grayed-out ini settings and the saved
version choice. The test fixtures now keep QSettings in a temporary folder, so the tests no longer write to the
user's own settings (or to the Windows registry).

Changelog: 09-10-2026 22:59:08

README: only the app's name, what it does and its license, with a link to its chapter of the manual; the rest moved into the manual (docs/apps/helixsr.md).

Changelog: 09-10-2026 18:32:48

Deploy page, OptiScaler folder mode: an optional second upscaler. Pick another FidelityFX upscaler DLL (for example
AMD's amd_fidelityfx_upscaler_dx12.dll with FSR 4) and it is copied into the folder as
amd_fidelityfx_upscaler_dx12.amd.dll, with [Forwarding] UpscalerDll in the folder's helixsr.ini pointing at it, so
OptiScaler's FFX Upscaler menu lists its upscalers after HelixSR (as HelixSR's README describes). Deploying again
without one removes it and clears UpscalerDll when it named it; removing the folder removes it too. HelixSR itself
is refused as the second upscaler. Help and README describe it; all languages updated.

README: tests run from the suite's ./test.sh and its CI, releases are tagged helixsr-v<x.y.z> in the suite.

Changelog: 09-10-2026 18:20:11

The app's own update check asked the old stand-alone repository, which no longer exists, so it reported "GitHub
answered 404". It now searches the BC250 Bazzite Suite releases for this app's tag (helixsr-v*), and says "no release
tagged helixsr-v* yet" until the first one. The Help page's source link opens the app's folder in the suite
repository, and the README's install steps use the suite's releases and repository.

Build time: the network build takes about 5-6 minutes on a BC-250 (HelixSR's setup compiles its shaders for both wave
sizes), not 2-3: the Setup page, Help and README say so, and the Setup page's stage line shows the elapsed time
while a job runs and how long it took afterwards. The header boxes (payload, network files, deployments, Steam
games) center their text. All languages updated; the .qm files are rebuilt with tools/compile_translations.py,
which reproduces the previous ones byte for byte.

Changelog: 09-10-2026 17:51:51

Only one instance runs per user: starting the app again brings the running window to the front instead of opening a second one (bc250_core's instance module, copied into the package until the app moves onto core).

